{"version":"6.9","hitCount":1,"request":{"queryString":"EXT_ID:9748275 AND SRC:MED","internalQuery":"EXT_ID:9748275 AND SRC:MED","resultType":"CORE","cursorMark":"*","pageSize":25,"sort":"","synonym":false},"resultList":{"result":[{"id":"9748275","source":"MED","pmid":"9748275","doi":"10.1074/jbc.273.40.25974","title":"Catabolism of phenylacetic acid in Escherichia coli. Characterization of a new aerobic hybrid pathway.","authorString":"Ferrández A, Miñambres B, García B, Olivera ER, Luengo JM, García JL, Díaz E.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Ferrández A","lastName":"Ferrández","firstName":"A","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Molecular Microbiology, Centro de Investigaciones Biológicas, Consejo Superior de Investigaciones Científicas, 28006 Madrid, Spain."}]}},{"fullName":"Miñambres B","lastName":"Miñambres","firstName":"B","initials":"B","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4590-4911"}},{"fullName":"García B","lastName":"García","firstName":"B","initials":"B"},{"fullName":"Olivera ER","lastName":"Olivera","firstName":"E R","initials":"ER"},{"fullName":"Luengo JM","lastName":"Luengo","firstName":"J M","initials":"JM","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4984-6256"}},{"fullName":"García JL","lastName":"García","firstName":"J L","initials":"JL"},{"fullName":"Díaz E","lastName":"Díaz","firstName":"E","initials":"E","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9731-6524"}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4984-6256"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9238-2485"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9731-6524"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2315-8524"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4590-4911"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","related_data","wikipedia"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"40","volume":"273","journalIssueId":934722,"dateOfPublication":"1998 Oct","monthOfPublication":10,"yearOfPublication":1998,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"J Biol Chem","title":"The Journal of biological chemistry","essn":"1083-351X","issn":"0021-9258","isoabbreviation":"J Biol Chem","nlmid":"2985121R"},"printPublicationDate":"1998-10-01"},"pubYear":"1998","pageInfo":"25974-25986","abstractText":"The paa cluster of Escherichia coli W involved in the aerobic catabolism of phenylacetic acid (PA) has been cloned and sequenced. It was shown to map at min 31.0 of the chromosome at the right end of the mao region responsible for the transformation of 2-phenylethylamine into PA. The 14 paa genes are organized in three transcription units: paaZ and paaABCDEFGHIJK, encoding catabolic genes; and paaXY, containing the paaX regulatory gene. The paaK gene codes for a phenylacetyl-CoA ligase that catalyzes the activation of PA to phenylacetyl-CoA (PA-CoA). The paaABCDE gene products, which may constitute a multicomponent oxygenase, are involved in PA-CoA hydroxylation. The PaaZ protein appears to catalyze the third enzymatic step, with the paaFGHIJ gene products, which show significant similarity to fatty acid beta-oxidation enzymes, likely involved in further mineralization to Krebs cycle intermediates. Three promoters, Pz, Pa, and Px, driven the expression of genes paaZ, paaABCDEFGHIJK, and paaX, respectively, have been identified. The Pa promoter is negatively controlled by the paaX gene product. As PA-CoA is the true inducer, PaaX becomes the first regulator of an aromatic catabolic pathway that responds to a CoA derivative. The aerobic catabolism of PA in E. coli represents a novel hybrid pathway that could be a widespread way of PA catabolism in bacteria.","affiliation":"Department of Molecular Microbiology, Centro de Investigaciones Biológicas, Consejo Superior de Investigaciones Científicas, 28006 Madrid, Spain.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","